<p>Asking for the business feels awkward mostly when it comes as a surprise. If every conversation has ended with an agreed next step, asking for a yes is just the last one.</p>

<h2>Signs they’re ready</h2>
<ul>
  <li>They ask about start dates, payment or what happens next.</li>
  <li>They talk as if it’s already happening: “when you come round…”.</li>
  <li>Their questions get specific and the objections run out.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to ask</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Summarise</strong> what they need and what you’ll do, in their words.</li>
  <li><strong>Ask plainly</strong>: <em>“Shall we go ahead?”</em> or <em>“Would you like me to book you in for the 14th?”</em></li>
  <li><strong>Then stop talking.</strong> Let them answer, however long the pause.</li>
</ol>

<h2>If they say yes</h2>
<p>Thank them, confirm the next step in writing, and press <strong>Make a client</strong> on the lead: it becomes a client with them as its contact, ready for the work.</p>

<h2>If they hesitate</h2>
<p>Treat it as an objection: <em>“What’s holding you back?”</em> (see <em>Handling objections</em>). If it’s not now, agree when to talk again and add the follow-up.</p>
